{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789821025,"nonce":"N+NCTq1Hos/67d6xSel66uLNGXg=","title":"A single long call runs in the interpreter until the LLVM tier delivers","message":"A function called once whose loop is hot has no call count to promote it.\nThe interpreter asks for promotion after 256 visits of a resumable loop\nheader, `ensure_baseline` finds the tier-0 body already compiled at load,\nand the request goes straight to the LLVM tier. Tier-0 helpers are not\npublished when a higher tier will publish its own, and tier 0 emits\nprobes rather than helpers anyway, so the interpreted frame can only\nleave through an LLVM helper. Until it lands the frame interprets at\nabout a microsecond per iteration.\n\nMeasured on a scimark-shaped SOR (100x100 array('d'), `sor(1.25, g, n)`\ncalled once from main): the inner header is rejected\n(RegionHasExternalEntry), so the request came from the middle loop after\n256 visits, 26 ms in; main's own LLVM compile occupied the promoter for\n65 ms; sor's took 35 ms more. The first 60 to 100 ms of every such call\nrun at interpreter speed, then 8 ns per inner iteration (PyPy's pace).\nFor n=10 that is the whole call: 98 ms against PyPy's 3 ms and CPython's\n31 ms. scimark_sor's SOR_execute is exactly this shape.\n\nDirections, in order of payoff:\n- count visits of every loop header for the request, including headers\n  with no resume point, so the ask comes from the innermost loop;\n- give an interpreted frame a Cranelift tier-1 resume point while LLVM\n  compiles (cranelift.resume_points already builds them on demand), with\n  a second helper slot so a frame resumed in Cranelift code can still\n  move on to the LLVM helper when it lands (a helper has no probes);\n- find where main's 65 ms LLVM compile goes.","files":null}]}